// Doc 87 §2.2: "Field access is `.` too, and a bare field reference is `(.name)`
// — resolved by the solver, which knows whether `name` is a field or a method."
//
// This is §1.2's inversion in the half that *needs* the solver. `x.f y` is a
// call whatever the types are, because a field access takes no arguments; `x.f`
// is a field read or a nullary method call and nothing syntactic separates them.
// Generation 1 could not choose, which is why plan 57 §1.1 made `.` field access
// and nothing else and `docs/DECLINED.md` recorded UFCS as "requires type
// knowledge to choose a shape". Lattice has the type knowledge.

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]
struct Cursor {
    pos: usize,
}

impl Cursor {
    pub fn depth(&self) -> usize {
        self.pos
    }

    pub fn doubled(&self) -> usize {
        self.pos * 2
    }
}

// `pos` is a declared field of `Cursor`, so this emits `c.pos` — a read.
fn held(c: &Cursor) -> usize {
    c.pos
}

// `depth` is an instance method of `Cursor`, so this emits `c.depth()` — a call.
// Same syntax, different lowering, and the solver is what decides which.
fn reached(c: &Cursor) -> usize {
    c.depth()
}

// Both in one expression, so the decision is per access and not per file.
fn summed(c: &Cursor) -> usize {
    c.pos + c.doubled()
}

// The default is **field**, and that is a deliberate restriction rather than an
// oversight. Outside a resolved scope — a caller that never ran the solver — every
// `.name` stays exactly what generation 1 emitted, so a missing answer degrades
// instead of turning every field read into a call that does not exist. It is the
// same rule the SOLVE-stage judge follows: speak only where sure.
//
// **A `self` receiver is not resolved either**, and that is the sharper limit.
// `self.depth` inside an `instance Cursor` method emits `self.depth` — a field
// read — because the solver does not bind `self` to the enclosing instance's
// target, so `receiver_owner` has nothing to answer with. A method calling a
// sibling method therefore still needs the receiver-last `.depth self`. Doc 97
// §10.36 records it with the test that pins it.
//
// **What the `std` boundary costs, stated precisely.** The declaration environment knows the
// fields and methods of types declared *here*, and nothing about `std`. So a
// receiver-first `v.len` on a `Vec` is not resolved to a method and emits `v.len`,
// which is `E0615`. Receiver-last `.len v` is unaffected — it was never a
// `Field` — so this line is the form that works today, and `v.len` is the form
// that waits on `std` reaching the environment. Doc 101 §10.8 records it.
fn counted(v: &Vec<u64>) -> usize {
    v.len()
}

fn main() {
    let c = Cursor { pos: 7 };
    println!("{} {} {}", held(&c), reached(&c), summed(&c));
    let v: Vec<u64> = Vec::new();
    println!("{}", counted(&v));
}
